bg.2018.2.6.reviewed_statement:
On a planet there are $M$ countries and $N$ cities. Two-way roads have been built between some of the cities. It is known that:

(1) every country has at least three cities;

(2) from every city in any country there are roads to at least half of the remaining cities in that country;

(3) from every city there is exactly one road to a city in some other country;

(4) between the cities of two different countries there are at most two roads;

(5) if two countries have together fewer than $2M$ cities, then there is at least one road between these two countries.

Prove that a traveller can visit at least $M + \frac{N}{2}$ cities, travelling only along the roads, and finally return to the city from which he started.

it.2013.0.1.reviewed_statement:
A model car is tested on some closed circuits $600$ metres long, made up of flat stretches and of uphill or downhill stretches. All uphill and downhill stretches have the same slope. The tests reveal some curious facts:

(a) the speed of the car depends only on whether it is going uphill, on the flat or downhill; calling these three speeds $v_s$, $v_p$, $v_d$ respectively, we have $v_s < v_p < v_d$;

(b) $v_s$, $v_p$ and $v_d$, expressed in metres per second, are integers;

(c) however the circuit is made up (with more or fewer climbs and descents), the time needed to complete it is always $50$ seconds.

Find all possible values of $v_s$, $v_p$, $v_d$.

it.2008.0.1.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABCDEFGHILMN$ be a regular dodecagon. Let $P$ be the intersection of the diagonals $AF$ and $DH$. Let $S$ be the circle through $A$ and $H$ that is congruent to the circumcircle of the dodecagon and distinct from it. Prove that:

(a) $P$ lies on $S$;

(b) the center of $S$ lies on the diagonal $HN$;

(c) the length of $PE$ equals the side of the dodecagon.

ee.2004.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $n$ and $c$ be coprime positive integers. For any integer $i$, let $i'$ denote the remainder of $ci$ upon division by $n$. Let $A_0A_1 \ldots A_{n-1}$ be a regular $n$-gon. Prove that

a) if the segments $A_iA_j$ and $A_kA_l$ are parallel, then the segments $A_{i'}A_{j'}$ and $A_{k'}A_{l'}$ are also parallel;

b) if the segments $A_iA_j$ and $A_kA_l$ are perpendicular, then the segments $A_{i'}A_{j'}$ and $A_{k'}A_{l'}$ are also perpendicular.

frt.2019.4.10.reviewed_statement:
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a circle of radius $1$ and let $T$ be a real number. A set of triangles is called $T$-daring if it satisfies the following three conditions:
- the vertices of each triangle lie on $\mathcal{C}$;
- the triangles have pairwise disjoint interiors (but two triangles may share a side or a vertex);
- each triangle has perimeter strictly greater than $T$.
Find all real numbers $T$ such that, for every integer $n\ge 1$, there exists a $T$-daring set containing exactly $n$ triangles.

hkt.2014.1.5.reviewed_statement:
Find the largest integer $n$ satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $n^2$ can be expressed as the difference of two consecutive cubes;

(ii) $2n + 79$ is a perfect square.

hkt.2015.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Find the maximum number of sets which simultaneously satisfy the following $3$ conditions:

(i) any of the sets consists of $4$ elements;

(ii) any two different sets have exactly $2$ common elements;

(iii) no two elements are common to all the sets.

si.2012.0.4.reviewed_statement:
The mole Črt has $6$ chambers in his burrow, numbered with the numbers from $1$ to $6$. Between some of them Črt has dug tunnels, so that from every chamber he can crawl through some tunnels to every other chamber. No two tunnels intersect. Every tunnel begins in some chamber, ends in some other chamber (different from the starting one), and does not pass through any chamber in between. Two chambers that are directly connected by a tunnel are called adjacent. List the pairs of adjacent chambers if the following holds:
- if Črt crawls through exactly three (not necessarily distinct) tunnels, he can get from chamber $1$ to chambers $1$ and $6$;
- if Črt crawls through exactly three (not necessarily distinct) tunnels, he cannot get from chamber $1$ to chamber $2$;
- if Črt crawls through exactly four (not necessarily distinct) tunnels, he cannot get from chamber $1$ to chamber $6$;
- if Črt crawls through exactly two tunnels, he cannot get from chamber $1$ to chamber $5$.

lv.2009.0.4.reviewed_statement:
On a certain planet $2009$ languages are used. Can a system of dictionaries be created so that the following $3$ properties hold simultaneously:
(a) each dictionary allows one to translate from one language into some other, but not in the opposite direction;
(b) if there is a dictionary that allows one to translate from some language $A$ into some other language $B$, then there is no dictionary that allows one to translate from $B$ into $A$;
(c) from every language into every other one can translate using either one or two dictionaries? (Repeated translations are allowed, for example, from $A$ into $B$ and further from $B$ into $C$.)

lv.2015.0.5.reviewed_statement:
In the Votivapu language all words consist only of the two letters $a$ and $b$. Every word can be obtained from the word "$a$" by repeatedly applying the following three rules:
1) appending the letter $b$ at the end of the word;
2) appending the word itself at its end;
3) replacing three consecutive letters $a$ in the word by one letter $b$.
Are the following words in the Votivapu language: (a) $abbababab$; (b) $baabaabaa$?

eet.2005.1.2.reviewed_statement:
On the planet Automory, there are infinitely many inhabitants. Every Automorian loves exactly one Automorian and honours exactly one Automorian. Additionally, the following can be noticed:
- each Automorian is loved by some Automorian;
- if Automorian $A$ loves Automorian $B$, then also all Automorians honouring $A$ love $B$;
- if Automorian $A$ honours Automorian $B$, then also all Automorians loving $A$ honour $B$.
Is it correct to claim that every Automorian honours and loves the same Automorian?

cyt.2016.2.5.reviewed_statement:
Someone fits four new tyres to his car and buys a new spare tyre. All five tyres are identical. If we know that the rear tyres of the car wear out after $42000$ km while the front ones wear out after $58000$ km, find:
(a) the maximum distance in km that the car can travel with these five tyres;
(b) propose a way of using the tyres so that the car travels the maximum distance using all five tyres.
